#ansible-vault create secrets.yml

Yes — by default, ansible-vault create secrets.yml will prompt you interactively for a password, twice (to confirm):

New Vault password: 
Confirm New Vault password:

After you enter and confirm it, it opens your default editor (usually vi, or whatever $EDITOR is set to) so you can type the plaintext content. Once you save and exit the editor, Ansible encrypts the file using that password.


# playbook.yml
Option 1 — using the dedicated MySQL module (preferred answer):

---
- name: deploy app
  hosts: appservers
  vars_files:
    - secrets.yml

  tasks:
    - name: connect to db
      community.mysql.mysql_db:
        login_user: admin
        login_password: "{{ db_password }}"
        name: mydb
        state: present
      no_log: true

--
#ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini playbook.yml --ask-vault-pass

Yes — --ask-vault-pass explicitly tells Ansible to prompt you interactively for the vault password before running the playbook:

Vault password:

You type the same password you used when creating/encrypting secrets.yml, hit enter, and then the playbook proceeds normally — Ansible decrypts the vaulted content in memory using that password.


